# Atomically Dispersed Binary Co‐Ni Sites in Nitrogen‐Doped Hollow Carbon Nanocubes for Reversible Oxygen Reduction and Evolution

## abstract
With the inspiration of developing bifunctional electrode materials for reversible oxygen electrocatalysis, one strategy of heteroatom doping is proposed to fabricate dual metal single-atom catalysts. However, the identification and mechanism functions of polynary single-atom structures remain elusive. Atomically dispersed binary Co-Ni sites embedded in N-doped hollow carbon nanocubes (denoted as CoNi-SAs/NC) are synthesized via proposed pyrolysis of dopamine-coated metal-organic frameworks. The atomically isolated bimetallic configuration in CoNi-SAs/NC is identified by combining microscopic and spectroscopic techniques. When employing as oxygen electrocatalysts in alkaline medium, the resultant CoNi-SAs/NC hybrid manifests outstanding catalytic performance for bifunctional oxygen reduction/evolution reactions, boosting the realistic rechargeable zinc-air batteries with high efficiency, low overpotential, and robust reversibility, superior to other counterparts and state-of-the-art precious-metal catalysts. Theoretical computations based on density functional theory demonstrate that the homogenously dispersed single atoms and the synergistic effect of neighboring Co-Ni dual metal center can optimize the adsorption/desorption features and decrease the overall reaction barriers, eventually promoting the reversible oxygen electrocatalysis. This work not only sheds light on the controlled synthesis of atomically isolated advanced materials, but also provides deeper understanding on the structure-performance relationships of nanocatalysts with multiple active sites for various catalytic applications.

With the inspiration of developing bifunctional electrode materials for reversible oxygen electrocatalysis, one strategy of heteroatom doping is proposed to fabricate dual metal single-atom catalysts. However, the identification and mechanism functions of polynary single-atom structures remain elusive. Atomically dispersed binary Co-Ni sites embedded in N-doped hollow carbon nanocubes (denoted as CoNi-SAs/NC) are synthesized via proposed pyrolysis of dopamine-coated metalorganic frameworks. The atomically isolated bimetallic configuration in CoNi-SAs/NC is identified by combining microscopic and spectroscopic techniques. When employing as oxygen electrocatalysts in alkaline medium, the resultant CoNi-SAs/NC hybrid manifests outstanding catalytic performance for bifunctional oxygen reduction/evolution reactions, boosting the realistic rechargeable zinc–air batteries with high efficiency, low overpotential, and robust reversibility, superior to other counterparts and state-of-the-art precious-metal catalysts. Theoretical computations based on density functional theory demonstrate that the homogenously dispersed single atoms and the synergistic effect of neighboring Co-Ni dual metal center can optimize the adsorption/desorption features and decrease the overall reaction barriers, eventually promoting the reversible oxygen electrocatalysis. This work not only sheds light on the controlled synthesis of atomically isolated advanced materials, but also provides deeper understanding on the structure–performance relationships of nanocatalysts with multiple active sites for various catalytic applications.
To date, large numbers of low cost and efficient catalysts have been constructed including transition metal (TM)/oxides/ nitrides/chalcogenides,[1] metal-free heteroatom-doped carbon materials,[2] and their coupled composites.[3] Among them, TM singleatom catalysts (SACs) have recently emerged as a new type of frontier materials with high activity, stability, and selectivity, rendering the great potential for diverse catalytic systems.[4] The unique electronic structure, maximized atomutilization efficiency, and unsaturated coordination bonds of the active centers in SACs contribute to the enhanced performance.[5] Moreover, recent investigations have demonstrated that the introduction of secondary metal atoms can further enhance the activity of SACs, indicating the promising development of dual-metal SACs.[6] Nevertheless, on the one hand, there is a serious lack of effective strategies to achieve the atomic control of targeted reactive sites comprising binary metal atoms; on the other hand, the identification of the diatomic structure in dual-metal SACs and the deeper functional mechanism of bimetallic atoms for synergistic catalysis are still in their infancy.
Owing to the increasing concerns from energy and environmental issues, growing attention has been paid on developing sustainable energy conversion and storage technologies, such as water-splitting electrolyzers, fuel cells, metal–air batteries, etc.[7] However, the sluggish kinetics of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) on the electrodes has been proven to be a complicated bottleneck of these renewable devices, remarkably resulting in high overpotential, low energy efficiency, and limited cycle life.[8] Noble metals and their alloys are still recognized as the best-known ORR/OER electrocatalysts, but the high cost and element scarcity seriously limit their widespread commercialization.[9] Moreover, these monofunctional precious metals as the catalytic materials fail to fulfill the requirements of simultaneously promoting reversible ORR and OER (e.g., Pt and its alloys are ORR active only while Ir/Ru-based materials are capable of catalyzing OER), dramatically hindering the construction of regenerative and rechargeable energy systems.[10] Therefore, exploring earth-abundant and highly active preciousmetal-free catalysts is urgently needed but still challenging. TM SACs have recently been recognized as one of the most promising candidates to replace Pt-based catalysts owing to their remarkable ORR activities.[11] However, although monometallic TM (e.g., Fe,[12] Co,[13] etc.) SACs have been widely employed for catalyzing ORR, their capabilities toward the reverse OER are unsatisfied, bringing a great challenge for the realization of efficient ORR/OER bifunctional catalysis.[14] A strategy of heteroatom doping was proposed to fabricate diatomic metal catalyst to potentially overcome this significant issue.[11c] Unfortunately, the controlled synthesis of binary metal SACs has seldom been investigated because of the great challenge in isolating and stabilizing single-atom active sites, especially involving dual metals. Meanwhile, the chemical interaction and synergistic effect of heterogenous bimetallic species for boosting the reversible ORR/OER still remain unclear. All these factors are the foundation of rationally designed SACs with optimized composition and structure as highly efficient and durable functional catalysts for chemical and electrochemical reactions.
Herein, we report the synthesis of a novel atomically dispersed binary Co-Ni sites embedded in nitrogen-doped hollow carbon nanocubes (denoted as CoNi-SAs/NC) by pyrolyzing the dopamine (DPA)-coated metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). The bimetallic Co-Ni atoms are identified by aberration-corrected scanning transmission electron microscopy (AC-STEM) and X-ray absorption (XAS) characterizations. Benefiting from the abundant single-atom active sites and the synergistic effect from diatomic structure in porous conductive carbon frameworks, the resultant CoNi-SAs/NC hybrid facilitates electrocatalytic ORR and OER by delivering low overpotential, high electron transfer number, and good reversibility in alkaline electrolyte, surpassing many of the reported precious metal-free electrocatalysts. Equipped in practical liquid and quasi-solidstate flexible Zn–air batteries, it also endows them with improved charge/discharge efficiency and prolonged cycling lifespan that are superior to those of counterparts and even precious metal catalyst of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ . Furthermore, theoretical calculations based on density functional theory (DFT) highlight the accessibility of atomically isolated catalytic sites with high reactivity and the synergistic interaction of dual metal-N structure (Co-Ni-N) in decreasing the energetic barrier and accelerating reaction kinetics in comparison to simple monometallic Ni-N and CoNi nanoparticle counterparts. We conclude that the precise control of polynary active sites at atomic level will open up new opportunities for rational design of high-performance materials based on earth-abundant elements for renewable energy conversion technologies.
The synthetic strategy of atomically dispersed binary Co-Ni sites in nitrogen-doped hollow carbon NCs catalysts could be divided into three steps, as illustrated in Figure  1. First, CoNi-MOFs were fabricated by chemical precipitation of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}{}^{3-}$ .[15] Subsequently, after 6 h magnetic stirring at alkaline $\mathrm{\pH}$ values, DPA could be self-polymerized to form polydopamine (PDA), which can uniformly cover all over the CoNi-MOFs to produce CoNi-MOFs/PDA core–shell NCs.[16] After annealing at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ atmosphere, these core–shell CoNi-MOFs/PDA NCs are carbonized and transformed into hollow CoNi particles encapsulated into nitrogen-doped hollow carbon NCs (denoted as CoNi-NPs/NC). In the last step, acid leaching treatment is employed to remove cobalt and nickel nanoparticles, and CoNi-SAs/NC can be finally obtained after washing and drying.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure S1, Supporting Information) images show the uniform NC sharp morphology of CoNi-MOFs and CoNi-MOFs/PDA products and the diameter is around $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After the pyrolysis process, massive cobalt and nickel nanoparticles aggregate on nitrogen-doped hollow carbon NCs, which can be seen from the X-ray diffraction pattern and SEM results (Figures S2 and S3, Supporting Information). The metal nanoparticles are removed after acid leaching and the CoNi-SAs/NC catalyst remains its original NC shape without any structural deterioration (Figure  2a,b). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figure 2c,d) reveal the hollow structure of CoNi-SAs/NC with shell thickness of near $30~\mathrm{nm}$ by the obvious contrast between solid shell and empty interior. Such a hollow NC design gives rise to high surface area and plentiful mesopores, which can shorten ionic/ electronic diffusion paths and provide efficient channels for mass transport.[17] Clear porous structure is also formed on the NC surface after the acid etching of the metal nanoparticles (Figure 2d). It should be noted that the ammonia atmosphere during the thermal treatment is essential for the generation of single-atom materials owing to its intrinsic reduction and etching functions, which possibly facilitate the subsequent removal of metal nanoparticles (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The absence of cobalt or nickel nanoparticles in CoNiSAs/NC is further confirmed by the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM, Figure 2e) and the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (the inset in Figure  2e). Aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark-field STEM (HAADF-STEM) image in Figure  2f clearly demonstrates the existence of isolated single-metal atoms, as marked by several red cycles. The corresponding elemental mapping images suggest the homogenous distribution of Co, Ni, N, and C elements over the whole hollow architecture (Figure  2g). The appearance of both Co and Ni signals evidences that diatomic sites are successfully embedded into carbon framework. To elucidate the contributions of metal species, pure N-doped carbon (NC) has also been synthesized by calcining the zeolitic imidazole frameworks without cobalt and nickel elements (Figure S5, see Experimental Section for synthetic details, Supporting Information).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was applied to characterize the surface structure and chemical composition of CoNi-NPs/NC and CoNi-SAs/NC. The XPS survey spectra in Figure S6a in the Supporting Information confirm the presence of C, N, Co, and Ni. The N 1s spectrum of CoNi-SAs/ NC reveals the existence of pyridinic N (398.2  eV), which serve as anchoring points for metal center atoms (Figure S6b, Supporting Information).[18] The Co 2p and Ni 2p spectra of CoNi-NPs/NC reveal the high-valence state of metal species (Figure S6c,d, Supporting Information), which is ascribed to the unavoidable surface oxidation of Co and Ni nanoparticles, consistent with the previous observations.[1e,10d] Synchrotron X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) measurements were performed to further identify the atomic and coordination configurations in synthesized samples. Figure 2h shows the Co K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) curves of CoNiSAs/NC and CoNi-NPs/NC. The higher near-edge absorption energy of the former than the latter implies that the Co single atoms in CoNi-SAs/NC are more positively charged than that in CoNi-NPs/NC, in accordance with former results.[19] Figure  2i displays the Fourier transform (FT) $k^{3}$ -weighted $\chi(k)$ -function curves of the Co K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra. The first major peak for CoNi-SAs/NC at ${\approx}1.49\mathrm{~\AA~}$ can be readily assigned to the dominant Co-N coordination that has been widely recognized in SACs.[19] Meanwhile, a second peak at ${\approx}2.22$ Å verifies the presence of metal–metal path,[6a] which cannot be found in pure Co-N-C and Ni-N-C,[4f,19] proving the formation of diatomic Co-Ni coordination (insets of Figure 2i show the proposed model), in agreement with recently reported Fe-Co and Ni-Fe dual sites.[6a,20] Compared to the case of CoNi-NPs/NC, no Co–Co path at around 2.07 Å is detected in CoNi-SAs/NC,[4c,21] excluding the possible structural model of Co–Co bonds. Similar results can be also obtained from Ni K-edge XANES and FT-EXAFS spectra (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Moreover, DFT simulation was performed to deduce the possible bimetal-N models based on the collected FT curves (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The calculated Co-Ni length of $2.32\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ fits well with the experimental value of $2.22\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , further corroborating the presence of dual-metal Co-Ni sites. These results demonstrate the successful synthesis of atomically dispersed binary Co-Ni configurations in CoNi-SAs/ NC, which possesses unique electronic structure and coordination environment, potentially facilitating the electrocatalytic processes.
Half-cell tests based on a three-electrode configuration were conducted to assess the ORR/OER bifunctional electrochemical performance of synthesized CoNi-SAs/NC catalyst. The counterparts NC, CoNi-NPs/NC, commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ were also evaluated for comparison. The oxygen reduction currents were collected by using rotational ring-disk electrodes in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution at different rotation rates. The negligible background currents in Ar-saturated solution evidence that the observed active currents in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated solution originate from electrocatalyzing oxygen reduction (Figure S9, Supporting Information). As shown in the linear sweep voltammetry (LSVs, Figure 3a and Table S1, Supporting Information), CoNi-SAs/NC exhibits outstanding activity in terms of high onset potential (0.88 V), high half-wave potential (0.76 V), and large limiting current density $(4.95\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ , which outperform that of bare NC $(0.76\mathrm{V},0.63\mathrm{V},3.10\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}},$ ), CoNi-NPs/ NC $(0.78\mathrm{~V},\ 0.64\mathrm{~V},\ 3.85\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}})$ , and even compete with that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(0.90\mathrm{~V},\ 0.82\mathrm{~V},\ 5.09\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}})$ . The presented parameters of CoNi-SAs/NC actually surpass most of the nonprecious metal-based catalysts previously reported in the literatures (Table S2, Supporting Information). Specifically, the inferior catalytic behaviors of bare NC indicate that metal-N-C sites rather than N-C sites facilitate the ORR process in our examination while the comparison between CoNi-NPs/ NC and CoNi-SAs/NC highlights the enhanced catalytic reactivity and exposed amount of catalytically metal active sites when decreasing the geometry size from nanoparticle to single atoms.[4c,19] Furthermore, ring and disk currents were simultaneously collected to calculate electron transfer number (n) and peroxide percentage. The superior ORR activity was also glanced from the calculated n value and yield of peroxide species. As illustrated in Figure  3b, the peroxide percentage of CoNi-SAs/NC is below $6\%$ and the monitored $n$ is above 3.9 over the potential range of $0.3{-}0.8\mathrm{V},$ revealing the high ORR selectivity and efficient $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transfer mechanism. The apparent four-electron pathway can be also confirmed by the Koutecky– Levich (K-L) determination (Figure S10, Supporting Information), which was obtained from the polarization curves at different rotating speeds. Moreover, the K-L plots for CoNi-SAs/ NC at different potential show the parallel fitting correlation (Figure S11, Supporting Information), implying its first-order reaction kinetics with respect to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration and a potential-independent electron transfer rate.[22] The calculated kinetic current densities $(J_{\mathrm{k}})$ of CoNi-SAs/NC, CoNi-NPs/NC, NC, and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ are determined to be 23.2, 14.5, 7.3, and $26.0\ m\mathrm{A\cm}^{-2}$ at $0.5\mathrm{V}$ (Table S1, Supporting Information), respectively, further evidencing the fast reaction kinetics on atomically dispersed Co-Ni sites of CoNi-SAs/NC hybrid catalyst. Apart from high ORR activity, CoNi-SAs/NC also possesses remarkable longterm catalytic stability. After a continuous chronoamperometric period of $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the ORR activity retention of CoNi-SAs/NC is much higher than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ( $90\%$ vs $80\%$ , Figure  3c). The wonderful catalytic durability is further corroborated by almost overlapped CV curves after extended 2000 cycles (the inset of Figure  3c). In order to estimate the fuel crossover effect of CoNi-SAs/NC, chronoamperometric responses for catalyzing ORR at $0.7\mathrm{V}$ were detected with the addition of $1\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol. As displayed in Figure S12 in the Supporting Information, the relative current density of CoNi-SAs/NC shows slight change while that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ drops sharply after injecting methanol, indicating the superior tolerance to methanol.
To fulfill the requirement of bifunctional electrodes for rechargeable metal–air batteries, the OER performance of as-synthesized catalysts was also evaluated. To reach an anodic OER current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure  3d), the required overpotential of CoNi-SAs/NC is $340~\mathrm{\mV}_{\mathrm{{i}}}$ , lower than that of CoNi-NPs/NC $(440~\mathrm{\mV})$ and even $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ $(400~\mathrm{mV})$ , demonstrating its superior activity for catalyzing OER in alkaline media. The corresponding Tafel slope for CoNi-SAs/NC is fitted to be $58.7~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ (Figure  3e), obviously smaller than that of CoNi-NPs/NC $(146.1\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1})}$ ) and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ $(83.1~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ ), revealing the favorable OER kinetics. The charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ values are characterized to be 1.1, 4.4, and $2.8\Omega$ for CoNi-SAs/NC, CoNi-NPs/NC, and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , respectively (Figure  3f), implying the most efficient charge transfer of CoNi-SAs/NC during the electrochemical processes, which is an important factor in facilitating the catalytic activity. LSVs, Tafel, and $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ results collectively demonstrate the high catalytic capability of diatomic CoNi-SAs/NC for catalyzing oxygen evolution, an indication that it can be employed as a promising bifunctional electrode material for rechargeable metal–air batteries. Furthermore, no obvious degradation can be observed on the chronopotentiometry response of CoNi-SAs/NC after continuous $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ polarization at $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ while that of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ increased about $50~\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ , indicating the satisfactory durability of the former for OER electrocatalysis. This can also be proved by the negligible degradation in OER polarization curves after accelerated 1500 cycles at a scanning rate of $300\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ (Figure  3h). Moreover, the original hollow NC structure and atomically dispersed metallic sites of CoNi-SAs/ NC can be maintained well after the continuous polarization (Figure S13, Supporting Information), further confirming the outstanding electrocatalytic durability. Electrochemical active surface areas for CoNi-SAs/NC, CoNi-NPs/NC, and NC are measured to be 10.2, 5.4, and $4.3~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S14, Supporting Information), respectively, suggesting that CoNi-SAs/ NC with isolated single atoms can provide more exposed electroactive sites for chem-adsorption of reactants and subsequent reactions. The overall reversible electrocatalytic ORR/OER performance of all prepared catalysts is analyzed by the oxygen electrode activity (ΔE), which is the difference between the ORR half-wave potential and the OER potential at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Surprisingly, CoNi-SAs/NC affords the same $\Delta E$ value of $0.81\mathrm{V}$ to that of state-of-the-art precious metal catalyst of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ir}{\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ (Figure  3i), which outperforms most of the reported highly efficient bifunctional catalysts based on nonprecious metal elements up-to-date (Table S2, Supporting Information).
In conclusion, we have put forward the controlled pyrolysis of DPA-coated MOFs strategy to synthesize a novel atomically dispersed Co-Ni dual sites anchored on nitrogen-doped hollow carbon NCs. The isolated bimetallic Co-Ni single atoms in CoNi-SAs/NC are identified by AC-STEM and surface-sensitive XAS techniques. The binary CoNi-SAs/NC catalyst delivers superior bifunctional activity, long-term durability and reversibility for bifunctional ORR/OER, and rechargeable Zn–air batteries, which even outperform those of transition- and precious-metal-based materials. Experimental and theoretical investigations collectively demonstrate that the uniformly isolated single atoms with high reactivity and the synergistic catalysis from dual-metal Co-Ni sites could reduce the energetic barrier and speed up the reaction kinetic, substantially contributing to the enhanced electrocatalytic activity. These findings will provide guidance on the further rational design and preparation of SACs with polynary active sites as well as the deeper understanding of synergistic effect for energy-related catalytic applications.
